This is a dynamic programming solution to the Longest Palindromic Substring problem. The algorithm maintains a 2D boolean matrix is_palindrome where is_palindrome[i][j] is true if and only if the substring s[i:j+1] is a palindrome. The variable max_length stores the length of the longest palindromic substring found so far and max_end stores the index of the end of this substring. The test for a palindrome is case and space insensitive and trivially handles odd length palindromes. The outer loop iterates over all possible lengths of substrings, starting from 0 and going up to the original string length. For each valid length, an inner loop tests all relevant substrings. The base cases involve single character string which can be easy to identify. The fill expressions work consistently across even and odd numbers of characters. The fill expression leads to less complicated logic than specialized code for odd and even lengths. It does not make unnecessary comparisons so it is efficient. The algorithm handles the computation of the table in a single traversal without visiting an earlier character earlier than necessary. It finds the palindromic substring without excessive work: it does not compute the table diagonally and the substrings used in comparisons grow as needed but are limited in length.```categoriespythonalgorithmprogramming
